# grid-region.rq — which band lines are INSIDE the ruled grid (loop P AXIOM,
# fixwave A: ink-witness refinement). Open-world derivation over the per-band
# evidence graph (the band is the closure boundary).
#
# A rule is INTERIOR iff it has ink on BOTH sides — tab:hasInkLeft/tab:hasInkRight,
# emitted by gridregion.py from the band's words (a char/word CENTER strictly left,
# resp. right, of the rule's x). A real column separator always has ink flanking it
# on both sides; a double-drawn section-border twin has NO ink on its own outboard
# side, however close it sits to another rule x. This replaces the earlier MIN/MAX-x
# "strictly between the outermost rules" test, which a double-drawn edge defeats:
# on the real CBH section the inner border twin sits strictly between the outer
# twin and the far border, so the old distance-based test wrongly admitted it as
# interior (R31: closure by PRESENCE, never distance — no tolerance survives a
# double-drawn edge). The MIN/MAX sub-select is gone; there is no numeric literal.
#
# A line is a grid line iff an interior rule's y-extent crosses its center. ?x is
# projected alongside ?idx so gridregion.interior_rule_xs can read this SAME query
# result for the set of ink-interior rule x's (fixwave A §2, weld_hrule_boxes'
# full-width test) — one AXIOM execution, two projections.
PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
SELECT DISTINCT ?idx ?x WHERE {
  ?line a tab:BandLine ; tab:lineIndex ?idx ; tab:lineCenterY ?cy .
  ?r a tab:RuleSpan ; tab:ruleX ?x ; tab:ruleTop ?rt ; tab:ruleBottom ?rb ;
     tab:hasInkLeft true ; tab:hasInkRight true .
  FILTER(?rt <= ?cy && ?rb >= ?cy)
}
